# The generated frontend bundle is compared **byte for byte** by
# `cargo run -p macast-frontend -- --check`, and the bundler always joins lines with LF
# (`source.lines()` drops any CR, then rejoins with '\n'). On a Windows checkout with
# `core.autocrlf=true` the committed file would arrive as CRLF, so the comparison failed and
# the Windows CI job went red while macOS and Linux passed (measured 2026-10-23, the Rust
# pipeline's first real run).
#
# Pinning the file to LF keeps the artifact identical on every platform. It is a generated
# file, so no editor's line endings are being overridden.
frontend/bundle.js text eol=lf

# Generated corpora are read by tests through `include_str!` and compared value by value,
# but pinning them keeps diffs readable and stops a CRLF checkout from changing bytes that
# some corpora carry as hex-encoded payloads.
macast-*/tests/golden/** text eol=lf
